# Notification Copy Is a User-Facing Contract

`NotificationKind`, job names, enum values, and database identifiers are never display copy.

## Push copy

- Every push-capable `NotificationKind` has an explicit human-readable title/body path.
- Never pass a raw kind (`checkin_reminder`, `followed_article`, etc.) as `sourceLabel`, title,
  subtitle, or body.
- `sourceLabel` is optional display context only (person/group/article name). Omit it when it adds
  no value; do not invent generic text such as "From notifications".
- Fallback copy must still be specific and readable. Never expose snake_case, camelCase identifiers,
  "Notification", "New activity", or serialized payload fragments.
- Keep lock-screen copy concise and understandable without opening the app.
- Name the action in title **and** subtitle: posted / checked in / replied / boosted / reposted /
  nudged. iOS Communication notifications replace the alert title with the actor name, so the
  subtitle is what preserves "Checked in:" vs a bare body preview. Action subtitles end with a
  colon when a preview follows. Body may carry a content preview.

## Tests

- Maintain a table-driven test over every `NotificationKind` that can reach APNs.
- Reject snake_case/internal names and generic fallback text in both title and body.
- Add a targeted assertion whenever a new notification kind or source-label branch is introduced.
- Preview representative pushes with and without optional actor/group/content context.

Notification database titles/bodies, in-app rows, websocket payloads, and APNs copy may have
different lengths, but they must describe the same event with the same user-facing terminology.
